  "overview": "Ashi Garami, meaning 'leg entanglement' in Japanese, is a fundamental position in the modern leg lock system where one practitioner controls their opponent's leg using their legs in a configuration that isolates the lower limb for attack. The position is characterized by the attacking practitioner lying on their side or back while using both legs to control one of the opponent's legs, creating a mechanical advantage that enables various leg-based submissions including straight ankle locks, heel hooks, and toe holds.\n\nAshi Garami serves as the foundation for the entire leg entanglement game and represents the entry point into more advanced leg lock positions such as Inside Ashi, Outside Ashi, Cross Ashi, and the Saddle position. The position provides moderate control while offering significant offensive opportunities, making it a crucial position for modern no-gi grappling and submission-only competition formats. The basic Ashi Garami configuration allows the top practitioner to maintain distance while setting up leg attacks, though it requires careful attention to reaping rules in IBJJF competition.\n\nUnderstanding Ashi Garami is essential for both offensive leg lock specialists and defensive practitioners who need to recognize and escape leg entanglements. The position has evolved significantly with the development of systematic leg lock approaches by instructors like John Danaher, and it now forms a cornerstone of elite-level no-gi competition strategy. Success in this position depends on precise leg configuration, hip control, and understanding the mechanical relationships that create finishing opportunities.",
